(7528116, 1203465, 97166, '<td valign="top">Wow. I had an 8 on this, for a brown. I don''t know if that''s robbery or a special kind of recognition ;)<br/> <br/>There''s a special kind of energy here that I really got behind.</td>', 'Wow. I had an 8 on this, for a brown. I don''t know if that''s robbery or a special kind of recognition ;) There''s a special kind of energy here that I really got behind.', '2017-08-10 13:30:08.000000', NULL, 0),
(7528404, 1203246, 59029, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club!  First off, Roger, congrats on the HM, this is a very good macro.  The beetle''s antennae make a great way to lead the eye into the pic and to that funny little face it has.  The ambient lighting is perfect and the comp dead simple but very effective.  Even the colours work perfectly with the green of the leaf being complementary to the orange eye area.  Great find, and well deserving of the high score *and HM in a FS no less.  Keep up the great work, and look forward to seeing more of your work in the future.<br/> <br/>Susan</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club!  First off, Roger, congrats on the HM, this is a very good macro.  The beetle''s antennae make a great way to lead the eye into the pic and to that funny little face it has.  The ambient lighting is perfect and the comp dead simple but very effective.  Even the colours work perfectly with the green of the leaf being complementary to the orange eye area.  Great find, and well deserving of the high score *and HM in a FS no less.  Keep up the great work, and look forward to seeing more of your work in the future. Susan', '2017-08-11 15:17:20.000000', NULL, 0),
(7528405, 1203250, 59029, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club!  This is a fascinating image, mostly because most wasps are yellowy and black, first time I''ve seen one with such colouration...so that definitely adds exotic appeal to the pic.  At first I thought it was challenging its reflection in a camera lens, but then read your notes.  Good idea to rotate the image to make the water look like something else, without losing the impact of the shot.  The settings are all spot on, ambient light seems OK and the composition works well in this square format.  And for what I believe to be your first entry here, a very respectable placing in a Free Study.  Keep up the good work and would like to see more soon,<br/> <br/>Susan</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club!  This is a fascinating image, mostly because most wasps are yellowy and black, first time I''ve seen one with such colouration...so that definitely adds exotic appeal to the pic.  At first I thought it was challenging its reflection in a camera lens, but then read your notes.  Good idea to rotate the image to make the water look like something else, without losing the impact of the shot.  The settings are all spot on, ambient light seems OK and the composition works well in this square format.  And for what I believe to be your first entry here, a very respectable placing in a Free Study.  Keep up the good work and would like to see more soon, Susan', '2017-08-11 15:21:57.000000', NULL, 0),
(7528407, 1203067, 59029, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club!  I am impressed by the amount of work you put into this shot, and how bummed out you may feel by its poor score.  <br/> <br/>Like the commenters, I see the landscape and am drawn in by it...I have to admit that your son, cute as he may be, is simply a distraction.  After all look at that gorgeous scenery he is competing with!  His body language seems to reflect that he just isn''t interested in what''s going on around him, so his disengagement comes across. The post work is nice but as you must realize by now, even the most painstaking amount of post won''t help an image that is weak. For me personally, bleachy clouds - like those to the right of the pic - are another distraction t draw my eye away from the mountains.<br/> <br/>Hope this has been useful, feel free to PM me<br/> <br/>Susan</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club!  I am impressed by the amount of work you put into this shot, and how bummed out you may feel by its poor score.   Like the commenters, I see the landscape and am drawn in by it...I have to admit that your son, cute as he may be, is simply a distraction.  After all look at that gorgeous scenery he is competing with!  His body language seems to reflect that he just isn''t interested in what''s going on around him, so his disengagement comes across. The post work is nice but as you must realize by now, even the most painstaking amount of post won''t help an image that is weak. For me personally, bleachy clouds - like those to the right of the pic - are another distraction t draw my eye away from the mountains. Hope this has been useful, feel free to PM me Susan', '2017-08-11 15:28:35.000000', NULL, 0),
(7528409, 1203413, 59029, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club!<br/> <br/>Dang I wish you''d had a bigger lens up when you saw the dolphin(?)...it could have been an amazing shot.  But with only 50mm to play with I am afraid that the beastie got lost in all the waves.  Not sure what could have caused that line, probably just light shifting around a bit.  Composition is only OK in my opinion, with the horizon perilously close to level and beastie close to centre.  Still a respectable finish for a FS entry.<br/> <br/>Feel free to PM me with any questions,<br/> <br/>Susan</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club! Dang I wish you''d had a bigger lens up when you saw the dolphin(?)...it could have been an amazing shot.  But with only 50mm to play with I am afraid that the beastie got lost in all the waves.  Not sure what could have caused that line, probably just light shifting around a bit.  Composition is only OK in my opinion, with the horizon perilously close to level and beastie close to centre.  Still a respectable finish for a FS entry. Feel free to PM me with any questions, Susan', '2017-08-11 15:35:24.000000', NULL, 0),
(7528507, 1203246, 185773, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by snaffles:</b></div><hr/><i> Greetings from the Critique Club!  First off, Roger, congrats on the HM, this is a very good macro.  The beetle''s antennae make a great way to lead the eye into the pic and to that funny little face it has.  The ambient lighting is perfect and the comp dead simple but very effective.  Even the colours work perfectly with the green of the leaf being complementary to the orange eye area.  Great find, and well deserving of the high score *and HM in a FS no less.  Keep up the great work, and look forward to seeing more of your work in the future.<br/> <br/>Susan </i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>Hi Susan,<br/>          Great to hear from you again. Thank you and I am really happy managing to rech 4th(HM). My best so far and Really very happy. I am particular of it''s antennae as they are very long, I position myself lower then the normal eye level shot. This allow me to express it very long antennae and very detailed face which basically where I want them to see the compound eyes. I feel the leaf is beeded to show the base of where it is resting on, I am pretty fortunate too as this is a rather big beetle thus I was able to capture the details of it''s compound eyes clearly. Thank you once again for your comments and valuable pointers. They have prove very useful, thank you Susan.<br/> <br/>Best regards,<br/> <br/>Roger New</td>', 'Originally posted by snaffles: Greetings from the Critique Club!  First off, Roger, congrats on the HM, this is a very good macro.  The beetle''s antennae make a great way to lead the eye into the pic and to that funny little face it has.  The ambient lighting is perfect and the comp dead simple but very effective.  Even the colours work perfectly with the green of the leaf being complementary to the orange eye area.  Great find, and well deserving of the high score *and HM in a FS no less.  Keep up the great work, and look forward to seeing more of your work in the future. Susan   Hi Susan,          Great to hear from you again. Thank you and I am really happy managing to rech 4th(HM). My best so far and Really very happy. I am particular of it''s antennae as they are very long, I position myself lower then the normal eye level shot. This allow me to express it very long antennae and very detailed face which basically where I want them to see the compound eyes. I feel the leaf is beeded to show the base of where it is resting on, I am pretty fortunate too as this is a rather big beetle thus I was able to capture the details of it''s compound eyes clearly. Thank you once again for your comments and valuable pointers. They have prove very useful, thank you Susan. Best regards, Roger New', '2017-08-12 12:43:26.000000', NULL, 0),
(7529654, 1202820, 98565, '<td valign="top">Hey - you entered! Wohoo!<br/>Plus we can rub shoulders now...isn''t that awesome???<br/> <br/></td>', 'Hey - you entered! Wohoo!Plus we can rub shoulders now...isn''t that awesome??? ', '2017-08-18 16:41:22.000000', NULL, 0),
(7530772, 1203456, 59029, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club!  Yes, a pic that definitely makes you wonder...who are they, where are they coming from, are they good or are they bad?  The mist adds a great otherworldly effect and helps add to the mystery as we can only see silhouettes.<br/> <br/>Now, why didn''t this image do better...well, your settings are spot on for a shot like this, but the comp is a little boring. 1/3 sky (and intriguing silhouettes) and 2/3 boring muddy field, where there is nothing happening, nothing to draw the viewer in.  I think that if you had cropped out the muddy field from that black pit running through the middle of it, and maybe played a little more in whatever pp software you use, that this would have been a much stronger image.<br/> <br/>Hope this helps, feel free to PM me with any questions.  <br/> <br/>Susan</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club!  Yes, a pic that definitely makes you wonder...who are they, where are they coming from, are they good or are they bad?  The mist adds a great otherworldly effect and helps add to the mystery as we can only see silhouettes. Now, why didn''t this image do better...well, your settings are spot on for a shot like this, but the comp is a little boring. 1/3 sky (and intriguing silhouettes) and 2/3 boring muddy field, where there is nothing happening, nothing to draw the viewer in.  I think that if you had cropped out the muddy field from that black pit running through the middle of it, and maybe played a little more in whatever pp software you use, that this would have been a much stronger image. Hope this helps, feel free to PM me with any questions.   Susan', '2017-08-24 22:32:58.000000', NULL, 0),
(7531637, 1203483, 59029, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club!<br/> <br/>For a capture this is a very well-done shot.  I used to know someone who also did precision parachuting in the manner you describe.  I love that the building on the right looks almost like a submarine and the paratrooper is coming down from the air in flight.  The red of his outfit and parachute immediately make him the centre of attention, and the spectators on the building look clustered by the railings in awe.  <br/> <br/>Nitpicks: there are two large dots of sensor dust in the sky above the building in the mid-right section of the photo, which could have been cloned out.  A little bit of pp could have helped pop the reds just a little bit more to give the photo more vividness.  Don''t be afraid to use pp to your advantage, just don''t go overboard with it!!!  Overall a very good capture, did respectably in the infamously tough FS challenge. Keep up the good work and hope to see more of your work soon.<br/> <br/>Susan</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club! For a capture this is a very well-done shot.  I used to know someone who also did precision parachuting in the manner you describe.  I love that the building on the right looks almost like a submarine and the paratrooper is coming down from the air in flight.  The red of his outfit and parachute immediately make him the centre of attention, and the spectators on the building look clustered by the railings in awe.   Nitpicks: there are two large dots of sensor dust in the sky above the building in the mid-right section of the photo, which could have been cloned out.  A little bit of pp could have helped pop the reds just a little bit more to give the photo more vividness.  Don''t be afraid to use pp to your advantage, just don''t go overboard with it!!!  Overall a very good capture, did respectably in the infamously tough FS challenge. Keep up the good work and hope to see more of your work soon. Susan', '2017-08-29 20:44:59.000000', NULL, 0),
(7534654, 1203376, 46918, '<td valign="top">Hello from the critique club:<br/> <br/>I like the use of colors on this....The only area I really don''t like is the upper left of the shot. I believe that it came out too dark....However you were dealing with a low light situation so you had to kind of go with it. Overall nice shot and I think the score reflects that</td>', 'Hello from the critique club: I like the use of colors on this....The only area I really don''t like is the upper left of the shot. I believe that it came out too dark....However you were dealing with a low light situation so you had to kind of go with it. Overall nice shot and I think the score reflects that', '2017-09-12 14:15:26.000000', NULL, 0),
(7537387, 1203238, 185717, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by snaffles:</b></div><hr/><i> Greetings from the Critique Club!  A very nicely seen and caught shot of someone who truly pimps his ride :-) Love the b/w and the pan makes the shot - the rest of the tuktuk is slightly blurry but his face, the important part, is in focus and he does look like he is trying to catch your eye.  Now as to why the low placing...the composition is okay but very safe....there are some bleachy areas of white ie the roof of the vehicle...and some people will simply not care for the panning effect. (Same goes for lots of zoomblur effects, anytime the shutter is open longer than usual and the camera is being moved). This candid moment also has a bit of a snapshot feel to it.  FS is very difficult to do well in because you can enter pictures of anything, and this pic simply failed to catch the votes.  <br/> <br/>Hope this critique has been of help, feel free to PM me<br/> <br/>Susan </i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>Thank you Susan, your feedback is much appreciated.</td>', 'Originally posted by snaffles: Greetings from the Critique Club!  A very nicely seen and caught shot of someone who truly pimps his ride :-) Love the b/w and the pan makes the shot - the rest of the tuktuk is slightly blurry but his face, the important part, is in focus and he does look like he is trying to catch your eye.  Now as to why the low placing...the composition is okay but very safe....there are some bleachy areas of white ie the roof of the vehicle...and some people will simply not care for the panning effect. (Same goes for lots of zoomblur effects, anytime the shutter is open longer than usual and the camera is being moved). This candid moment also has a bit of a snapshot feel to it.  FS is very difficult to do well in because you can enter pictures of anything, and this pic simply failed to catch the votes.   Hope this critique has been of help, feel free to PM me Susan   Thank you Susan, your feedback is much appreciated.', '2017-10-01 22:01:48.000000', NULL, 0);
